  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/censorcreate#CL_HKR_Irony">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/censorcreate#Claim"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual"/>
    <aboutCensorshipEvent rdf:resource="http://example.org/censorcreate#EV_HKR_PrintApproval"/>
    <dataProvenance>documented case</dataProvenance>
    <supportedBy rdf:resource="http://example.org/censorcreate#SRC_Lance_Metailie"/>
    <supportedBy rdf:resource="http://example.org/censorcreate#SRC_Darnton1991"/>
    <confidenceLevel>medium</confidenceLevel>
    <claimText>The preface to the French edition argues that Braun uses the ruse and irony of Enlightenment writers (Diderot's Jacques le fataliste) to pass subversive ideas; the narrator explicitly thematises why so much 'cannot be said directly'. This is an interpretation by the preface author, and the novel was written (1981) before the formal review began.</claimText>
    <aboutCreativeResponse rdf:resource="http://example.org/censorcreate#RE_HKR_IronicNarration"/>
  </rdf:Description>
